<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1965 CE · capital Bathurst</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's smallest African colony, a narrow strip along the Gambia river with its capital at Bathurst, which became independent as The Gambia in 1965.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1816 Captain Alexander Grant founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Bathurst</a> on St Mary's Island at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> river, to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and secure British trade after the loss of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goree">Goree</a>. From 1821 the settlement was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-west-africa">British West Africa</a>, becoming a separate colony in 1843.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> was again placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> in 1866 and became separate for good in 1888. An Anglo-French agreement of 1889 fixed its borders as a strip a few kilometres wide on either side of the river, enclosed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the interior followed in 1894. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">Groundnuts</a> were the main export, and most people were Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Constitutional reforms in the 1950s and early 1960s brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-the-gambia">People's Progressive Party</a> to power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> became independent on 18 February 1965, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Bathurst</a> was renamed Banjul in 1973.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gambia-colony-and-protectorate.jpg" alt="Gambia Colony and Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_The_Gambia_(1889%E2%80%931965).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1965 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bathurst</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander Grant, Dawda Jawara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Mandinka, Wolof, Fula</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Gambia, The Gambia (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=gambia-colony-and-protectorate" aria-label="Gambia Colony and Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4226 1677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gambia Colony and Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4546 1933L4547 1931M4546 1933L4997 651M4546 1933L4634 2117"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4547 1931h0M4997 651h0M4634 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4546 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4546 1933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1893&amp;focus=gambia-colony-and-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
